e-way ANOVA table.\nSource Degrees of\nf reedom\nSum of Squares Mean Sum of\nSquares\nf\nBetween groups 3 0.004235 0.001411\nWithin groups 20 0.004625 0.000231 6.10\nTotal 23 0.008860\na. What hypotheses are being tested here?\nb. Interpret the results.\n3. In general, how does the F statistic as an analysis of variance distinguish between the\nnull and alternative hypotheses?\n4. A study is completed on the software development time for implementing an\nalgorithm programmed by 24 programmers using four different programming\nlanguages. The research question is “Does the choice of programming language\naffect software development time?” The 24 programmers were randomly assigned\nto program the algorithm in four different languages (A, B, C, and D). The software\ndevelopment times, in minutes, were taken and tested in random order. The analysis\nin Table 31.10 is exactly justified if the data are random samples from four normal\npopulations. Note that test order is listed in parentheses.\nTable 31.10 Programmer software development times by treatments.\nProgramming Language (Treatment)\nA b C D\n63 (12) 62 (20) 68 (16) 56 (23)\n67 (9) 60 (2) 66 (7) 62 (3)\n71 (15) 63 (11) 71 (1) 60 (6)\n64 (14) 59 (10) 67 (17) 61 (18)\n65 (4) 68 (13) 63 (22)\n66 (8) 68 (21) 64 (19)\n63 (5)\n59 (24)\na. State the formal hypothesis being tested.\nb. Are the differences between the treatment averages greater than expected given\nthe variation within treatments? (Hint: Run a one-way ANOVA.)\n400 n n n Resea R ch Methods fo R i nfo RM ation s yste M s n CH APTER 31\nc. Plot the t points for the four treatments on a common t\n20\ndistribution plot.\nUsing this plot as a basis, could these four t values be drawn randomly from a t\n20\ndistribution plot?\n5. Given the data in Table 31.11:\nTable 31.11 Artificial data for one-factor model.\nLevels of f actor A\n1 2 3 4"],[36255,"CHAPTER 31 n o ne-Way a nalysis of Va R iance n n n 401\nTable 31.12 Continued.\nLevels of f actor A\n1 2 3 4 5"],[36169,"Chapter 31 One-Way Analysis of Variance"],[38768,"Chapter 31, so I am\nleaving out the details\nof the simulation and\nfocusing only on the\nresults.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Programming and Design Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n361\n30\nSpreadsheets for\nSimulation\nGet your facts first. Then you can distort them\nas you please."],[38771,"Chapter 31.) First, I’ll discuss some useful elements of spreadsheets to get you started.\nBasics\nIn this section, I’ll cover basic nomenclature and use. If you are well-versed in Excel,\nyou should skim the rest of the chapter.\nEach rectangle in Excel is known as a cell, and each cell holds a discrete piece of data,\nsuch as a name or a number. By placing like cells next to each other, you can relate cells\ntogether. Generally, a row of cells describes some instance (such as a task, customer, or\nfeature) and columns describe elements of that instance. FIGURE 30.1 is part of an Excel\ntable of customers. One row is a customer. Each column is a feature of that customer.\nOften, you don’t have a unique identifier for every row, as in Figure 30.1. Sometimes you\nhave to supply that yourself. In database language, this is known as a primary key. It’s an\nidentifier for every row that is unique. For instance, names are considered poor candi-\ndates for primary keys because two people can have the same name (for example, which\nJohn Smith did you mean?), so it’s common to assign a unique number to every row."],[36269,"chapter 32\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[58465,"Chapter 32 of the book website at http://book.prototools.net.\nAfter you've prepared your project folder, open __Prospector_Scene_0 in Unity to get\nstarted.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n972 CHAPTER 34 PROSPECTOR SOLITAIRE – Part 2\nAdditional Prospector Game Elements\nNow that you can play through an entire round of the game, it's time to add the game\nelements that will take this from a rough prototype to something that you can play over\nand over. These elements include:\n■ The game knowing when a round has ended and whether the player won.\n■ A nice background and other game art.\n■ Keeping score and granting bigger scores for longer runs of cards (without\nclicking the draw pile).\n■ A GUI to show the score to the player.\n■ Animating the movement of the cards so that the game looks nicer.\n■ Animating the movement of the score to help players intuitively understand how the\nscore is calculated.\nManaging Prospector Rounds\nFirst up is making the game know when it's over. This only needs to be checked once\nafter each time the player has clicked a card, so the check will be called from the end of\nProspector.&$RDB&/I&.ED().\n1. Open the Prospector script in Visual Studio and add the new &hecN)orGaPe2Yer()\nand GaPe2Yer() methods between SetMine)ace8ps() and the static &$RDB\n&/I&.ED() method shown in Code Listing 34.1 as well as the single bold line at the\nend of &$RDB&/I&.ED().\nCode Listing 34.1 Prospector.cs\n| public class Prospector : MonoBehaviour {\n| …\n|\n| public void SetMineFaceUps() { … }\n|\n|\n> /// \u001fsuPPary>\n> /// Test Zhether the gaPe is oYer\n> /// \u001f/suPPary>\n> Yoid &hecN)orGaPe2Yer() ^ // a\n> // If the Pine is ePpty\u000f the gaPe is oYer\n> if ( Pine.&ount \u0013 ) ^\n> GaPe2Yer( true ); // &all GaPe2Yer() Zith a Zin\n> return;\n> }\n>\n> // If there are still cards in the Pine draZ pile\u000f the gaPe s not oYer\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\naddItIonal PROSPECTOR Game elements 973\n> if ( draZPile.&ount > \u0013 ) return;\n>\n> // &hecN for rePaining Yalid plays\n> foreach ( &ardProspector cp in Pine ) ^\n> // If there is a Yalid play\u000f the gaPe s not oYer\n> if ( target.$dMacentTo( cp ) ) return;\n> }\n>\n> // Since there are no Yalid plays\u000f the gaPe is oYer\n> GaPe2Yer( false ); // &all GaPe2Yer Zith a loss\n> }\n|\n|\n> /// \u001fsuPPary>\n> /// &alled Zhen the gaPe is oYer. SiPple for noZ\u000f but e[pandable\n> /// \u001f/suPPary>\n> /// \u001fparaP naPe \u0005Zon\u0005>true if the player Zon\u001f/paraP>\n> Yoid GaPe2Yer( bool Zon ) ^\n> if ( Zon ) ^\n> Debug./og( \u0005GaPe 2Yer. <ou Zon\u0004 \u001d)\u0005 );\n> } else ^\n> Debug./og( \u0005GaPe 2Yer. <ou /ost. \u001d(\u0005 );\n> }\n>\n> // Reset the &ardSpritesS2 singleton to null\n> &ardSpritesS2.RESET(); // b\n> // Reload the scene\u000f resetting the gaPe\n> // 1ote that there are T:2 underscores at the beginning of \u0005BBProspectorڕ"],[58348,"Chapter 32 of the book website at http://book.prototools.net.\nGetting Started: Space SHMUP – Part 2\nAt the end of the previous chapter, you had a pretty basic prototype of a space shooter.\nIn this chapter, you'll make it more fun and expansive. You will need to complete the\nproject from the previous chapter before you can begin this chapter.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n810 CHAPTER 32 SPACE SHMUP – Part 2\nEnemy to Enemy_0\nIn the previous chapter, your prefab for the enemy is called Enemy_0, but you're cur-\nrently using the Enemy class to control it. There are several instances when you might\nwant to know whether a GameObject is specifically an Enemy_0 and not some other\nkind of enemy (e.g., if your player earns a different number of points for different kinds\nof enemies), so we need a specific Enemy_0 script.\n1. Create an Enemy_0 script and attach it to the Enemy_0 prefab:\na. Create a new C# script named Enemy_0 in the __Scripts folder.\nb. Drag this Enemy_0 script onto the Enemy_0 GameObject prefab in the _Prefabs\nfolder. (If you're using Plastic SCM, you may need to first select the Enemy_0\nprefab in the Project pane and then click Checkout in the Inspector.)\nc. Select the Enemy_0 GameObject prefab in the _Prefabs folder and confirm that\nthe Enemy_0 (Script) component was attached (it will be at the bottom of the\nInspector).\n2. Remove the existing Enemy (Script) component from the Enemy_0 prefab.\na. Select the Enemy_0 GameObject prefab in the _Prefabs folder.\nb. In the Inspector, click the three vertical dots ( ) in the top-right of the Enemy\n(Script) component and choose Remove Component.\nNow you need to edit the Enemy_0 script to have exactly the same behavior as its super-\nclass Enemy.\n3. Open the Enemy_0 script in Visual Studio and make the bold code changes shown in\nCode Listing 32.1 (delete everything that's not in Code Listing 32.1).\nCode Listing 32.1 enemy_0.cs\n| using System.Collections;\n| using System.Collections.Generic;\n| using UnityEngine;\n|\n> public class Enemy_0 : EnePy { // EnePyB\u0013 e[tends the EnePy class // a\n> // Yoid Start() ^ } // Delete eYerything inside the class definition\u0004 // b\n> // Yoid 8pdate() ^ }\n| }\na. Replacing MonoBehaYiour with EnePy on this line is critical. This is what\nmakes EnePyB\u0013 a subclass of EnePy.\nb. You also must delete the Start() and 8pdate() methods so that they don't\noverride those in the Enemy superclass.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\nProGrammInG other enemIes 811\nBy making EnePyB\u0013 a subclass of EnePy (point // a in Code Listing 32.1), the EnePyB\u0013\nclass inherits all the non-private methods, fields, properties, and so on of the superclass\n(which is EnePy in this case).\n4. Save the Enemy_0 script, return to Unity, and click Play. You'll see that the Enemy_0"],[36170,"Chapter 32 Two-Way Analysis of Variance"],[58089,"Chapter 32, \"Space SHMUP – Part 2,\" covers how to set the Script Execution\nOrder in Unity to avoid race conditions.\nClass Inheritance\nClasses in C# very often extend other classes (i.e, they are based on other classes). In\nCode Listing 26.1, EnePy extends MonoBehaYiour, as do all the classes you've seen so\nfar in this book. Implement the following instructions to get EnePy working in your\ngame, and then we'll discuss this further.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n534 CHAPTER 26 Classes\nImplementing the Enemy Class on a GameObject\nComplete the following steps:\n1. Create a new scene (File > New Scene from the Unity menu bar). When asked, choose\nthe Basic (Built-in) scene template, which includes a Camera and a Directional Light.\n2. Save this new scene as _Scene_1 (File > Save As… from the Unity menu bar).\n3. Create a new sphere in the scene (GameObject > 3D Object > Sphere).\na. Rename the Sphere to EnemyGO (the GO stands for GameObject). This new\nsphere is not connected in any way to the Sphere in _Scene_0. (For example, it\ndoesn't have the CountItHigher or MoveAlong script components attached.)\nb. Set the transform.position of EnemyGO to [ 0, 4, 0 ] using the Transform compo-\nnent in the Inspector.\nc. Drag the Enemy script you wrote earlier in Code Listing 26.1 from the Project\npane onto EnemyGO in the Hierarchy pane of _Scene_1.\nd. Select EnemyGO in the Hierarchy, and you will see that Enemy (Script) now\nappears as a component of the EnemyGO GameObject. This component also\nshows the two public fields of the EnePy class, the floats speed and fireRate,\nthough as with the name of the component, capitalization and spaces are\nadded to field names in the Inspector.\n4. Drag EnemyGO from the Hierarchy pane into the Project pane to create a prefab\nnamed EnemyGO. As described in previous chapters, you'll know that the prefab\nwas created successfully because an item named EnemyGO with a blue box icon will\nappear in the Project pane, and the name of the EnemyGO GameObject in the Hier-\narchy pane will turn blue, indicating that it's an instance of the EnemyGO prefab.\n5. Select the Main Camera in the Hierarchy and set its Transform and Camera compo-\nnent settings to those highlighted by lighter areas in the left side of Figure 26.3:\na. In the Transform component of the Inspector, set the position to [ 0, -15, -10 ].\nb. In the Camera component of the Inspector:\nQ Set the camera Clear Flags to Solid Color.\nQ Change the camera Projection from Perspective to Orthographic.\nQ Set the camera Size to 20 (Size only appears for Orthographic cameras).\nThe Game pane shown at the right of Figure 26.3 should approximate what you now see\nthrough the camera, with the EnemyGO sphere right at the center top of the screen.\n6. Click Play. You should see the EnemyGO move down the screen at a constant rate."],[36359,"chapter 33\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[36171,"Chapter 33 Analysis of Covariance"],[58768,"Chapter 33 for examples of using JsonUtility. You should also look at the Unity API\ndocumentation.\n6\n6. https://docs.unity3d.com/2020.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/JsonUtility.html\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\nC# and Unity Coding Con C epts 27\nLambda Expressions =>\nA lambda expression is best thought of as short-hand for a simple, anonymous function\n(i.e., a function for which you don't need a name). You can see an example in the \"Inter-\nfaces\"\n7\nsection of this appendix as well as in Pseudocode Listing 28.13 of Chapter 28,\n\"Data-Oriented Design.\" Figure B.3 and the following bulleted list show the standard\nsyntax for a lambda expression.\nFigure B.3 Lambda Expression syntax\n■ Parameters: One or more parameters are passed in, surrounded by parentheses\n() and separated by commas. If there is only one parameter, parentheses are not\nnecessary.\n■ =>: The \"fat arrow\" separates the parameters from the operations.\n■ Operations: The operations of the lambda expression are surrounded by braces {}.\nIf there is only one statement, the braces are not required.\n■ Result: The result of the operations is returned to the left side of the = sign. If there\nis more than one statement in the Operations section, a return keyword is required\nto return a value (see // b in Code Listing B.17).\ntip\nFIELD VALUES COPIED FROM INSPECTOR COMPONENTS ARE JSON.\nWhen you right-click on any individual field shown in the Unity Inspector\n(including Lists and arrays) and then choose Copy, the information that is\ncopied is in JSON format, so you can paste it into a standard text editor. In\nfact, you can past it there, modify it, copy it again, and then right-click the\nfield in the Inspector and choose Paste to paste the modified values back\nonto the Component. I have found this to be especially useful when working\nwith [System.Serializable] classes in the Inspector.\n(Note: This only works on a single field, though that field can be a List or\narray of serializable classes. However, when you click the three vertical dots\n(\n… ) in the upper-right corner of a Component and choose\nCopy Component, it\nis not copied as JSON.)\n7. On line // c of Code Listing B.12.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n28 A PPENDIX B Usef U l Con C epts\nYou may be wondering what defines the types of the parameters and the return type\nof the result. This information comes from the delegate type that is targeted by the\nlambda expression. Code Listing B.17 shows some lambda expressions that target the\nFloatOpDelegate delegate type (from Code Listing B.6 of the \"Delegates\" section of\nthis appendix).\nCode Listing B.17 l ambda e xpressions and d elegates\n| using UnityEngine;\n|\n| public class LambdaExample : MonoBehaviour {"],[58841,"Chapter 33, \" Prospector Solitaire — Part 1,\" where I both show the layout of the game\nand explain two potential runs of cards that the player could choose. Anywhere that\nit might help, students should use diagrams.\n■ Examples of play: In addition to describing game mechanics, it also helps to have\nstudents give gameplay examples. In these examples, the reader is given a specific\ngameplay example and shown how it plays out. These are usually something like:\nExample: On his turn, Adam moves to the same table as Heidi and Ozzie. Instead\nof taking a basic Steal Action, he plays the Tag Team Takeout card from his hand.\nThe text on Tag Team Takeout reads \"Action: When you play this card, if you make\na successful Steal Roll, ALL monkeys at your table gain the results of your roll, and\nthen you gain duplicates of any items they gained this turn.\" Adam makes a suc-\ncessful Steal Roll for five Sugar Packets, causing him, Heidi, and Ozzie to each gain\nfive Sugar Packets from the bank. Then Adam also gains two Watermelon Slices\nand one Pie from the bank because on their turns Heidi had gained two Water-\nmelon Slices and Ozzie had gained one Pie.\nGameplay examples like this give designers the opportunity to show how the rules come\ntogether in an actual round of play and can help players understand the rules better.\nIf your students include all of these, their rules will be much better for it.\nRoleplaying Game Project\nNothing teaches adaptability in design like running a pen-and-paper roleplaying game.\nYou can look at the \"Pen-and-Paper Role-Playing Games\" section of Appendix B, \"Useful\nConcepts\" for my recommendations on how students should approach them. In terms\nof the assignment, I try to make groups of four where at least one person in the group\nhas played a paper roleplaying game before.\n7\nI ask the experienced student to be the\nfirst to run their game for their peers.\nTo kick off the RPG Project, I usually bring in student volunteers who have taken the\nclass before and run through the first scene of an RPG scenario that I developed with\n7. If you use the team shuffler from Table D.2, you can ask who has ever played a paper RPG\nin a poll at the beginning of class and then make that one of your \"roles\" for randomizing\nthe teams. If the first member of each Project 1 group has prior RPG experience, then when\nyou get to the RPG Project, the first member of each of those groups will have prior RPG\nexperience.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\nTeaching i n T roduc T ion T o g ame d esign 9\nme running the game as the Game Master (GM). After the first scene, I and each of\nthe student volunteers each take one of the RPG project groups and run the rest of the\nscenario with the members of the project groups each taking on one of the roles. At the"],[36418,"chapter 34\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[36172,"Chapter 34 Experimental Designs\n384 n n n Resea R ch Methods fo R i nfo RM ation s yste M s n u NIT 7\nLet’s study Winning Database Configurations: An IBM Informix Database Survey, by Marty\nLurie, IBM Software Group at www.ibm.com/developerworks/db2/zones/informix/library/\ntecharticle/lurie/0201lurie.html one more time. Lurie uses a regression model, at first, to\nlook for any statistical relationship between 7.x and 9.x servers. Study the ANOVA analysis.\n1. In this study how was ANOVA employed? What were the results?\n2. Design a two-way ANOVA design and data table for this study. What would you\nchoose for the dependent variable? Why? Describe your test for interaction.\n3. Is there an appropriate ANOCOVA design to consider?\n4. What type of sampling method was employed in this study? Does the answer to this\nquestion have an impact on what type of experimental design should be utilized?\n5. For you designs in problems 2, 3, and 4: develop linear regression models which will\ngenerate equivalent analyses as the ANOVA designs.\n6. Consider experimental designs which would control for indexing, partitioning, the\nNOAGE parameter, forced residency, and/or the fill factor parameter.\nchapte R 31\nO VERVIEW AND L EARNING Obj ECTIVES\nIn This Chapter\n31.1 i ntroduction\n31.2 s ample s tudy\n31.3 i ntroduction to ano V a\n31.4 t ests of h omogeneity of Variance"],[32098,"Chapter 34 of the IBC [12] provides direction for repairs, additions, and occupancy changes to\nexisting structures. In general, modifications to existing structures must comply with the design\nrequirements for new construction, and any modification should not cause any part of the existing\nstructure to be in violation of the requirements for new construction. Unaffected portions of the\nexisting structure are not required to comply with the current code requirements unless an existing\nmember is found to be structurally inadequate, in which case it must be modified or replaced to con -\nform to the current code. Another key provision is that modifications to an existing structure shall\nnot increase the stress to an existing member by more than 5% unless the member can adequately\nsupport the increased stress, and the strength of any existing member shall not be decreased by\nmore than what is required for new construction. An existing structure does not have to be inves -\ntigated for seismic loads if the addition to the structure is seismically independent of the existing\nstructure. If this is not the case, then the existing vertical lateral force resisting systems (LFRS)\nmust conform to the requirements for new structures unless the following conditions are met:\n• The addition must conform to the requirements for new construction;\n• The addition does not increase the stress in any member in the vertical LFRS by more\nthan 10% unless the member is adequate to support the additional stress; and\n• The addition shall not decrease the strength of any member in the vertical LFRS by\nmore than 10% unless the member is adequate to support the additional stress.\nOnce a new occupancy or proposed structural modification is defined, an investigation of\nthe existing structural condition is required. If the existing construction drawings are avail -\nable, the investigation is simpler; however, drawings for older structures are often not available\nand a more extensive field investigation is required. For any structural modification, a field\ninvestigation is usually necessary to help assess practical issues, such as the constructability of\nan addition or alteration. There are often physical barriers to structural modifications, such as\nmechanical equipment, lights, ducts, and other similar elements.\nOnce a structure is examined for adequacy, the nature of the required reinforcement can\nbe selected. For floor or roof systems that require a relatively large increase in load-carrying\ncapacity, the addition of new intermediate beams between existing beams may be required as\nshown in Figure 14-27. This scheme also decreases the span of the deck system and therefore\nincreases the load-carrying capacity of the slab system, which may be desirable in some cases.\nNote that the constructability of this scheme must be carefully considered. There will likely be"],[36456,"CHAPTER 35 n Rando M nUMB e R Gene R ation n n n 453\nThe desirable properties for a random number generator are:\nn The related software should execute efficiently.\nn The sequence should possess a large period.\nn The numerical values in the sequence should be independent and uniformly\ndistributed.\nThe last property requires a battery of statistical tests to validate randomness.\nSome of the random number generators commonly used include:\nn Linear congruential generators\nn Extended Fibonacci generators\nn Combined generators\nA description of each of these approaches follows."],[36461,"CHAPTER 35 n Rando M nUMB e R Gene R ation n n n 455\nn Exclusive-or random numbers obtained by two or more generators.\n2\nn Shuffle. Shuffling is when one sequence is used as an index to decide which of several\nnumbers generated by a second sequence should be returned.\n3\nAlgorithm M by\nMarsaglia and Bray uses an array of size 100 that is filled with random numbers from\na random sequence X\nn\n. To generate a random number, generate a new Y\nn\n(between\n0 and m – 1) and scale it to obtain an index I = 1 + 100 Y\nn\n/ m. The value in the i th\nelement of the array is returned as the next random number. A new value of X\nn\nis\ngenerated and stored in the i th location. One problem with shuffling is that it is not\neasy to skip a long subsequence as is often required in applications."],[36465,"CHAPTER 35 n Rando M nUMB e R Gene R ation n n n 457\nReferences\n1. L’Ecuyer, P., “Efficient and Portable Combined Random Number Generators,”\nCommunications of the ACM, 31 (1988): 742–749 and 774.\n2. Santha, M. and Vazirani, U.V., “Generating Quasi-Random Sequences from Slightly Random\nSources, ” Proc. 25\nth\nAnnual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (1984):\n434–440.\n3. Marsaglia, G. and Bray, T.A., “A Convenient Method for Generating Normal Variables,”\nSIAM Review, 6 (1964): 260–264."],[36437,"Chapter 35 Random Number Generation"],[36468,"Chapter 35, “Random Number Generation.”\n36.2 t he i nverse t ransformation\nSuppose we want to generate random values from A to B from a uniform distribution defined\nover [A, B]. We recall that the cumulative distribution function (CDF) in this case is mono-\ntonically increasing and continuous over the interval and is defined as:\nP x\nx A\nB A\n( ) , =\n−\n−\nfor A ≤ x ≤ B.\nThen in the inverse method we set r, a uniform value defined on [0, 1], equal to P(x), giving:\nr P x\nx A\nB A\n= =\n−\n−\n( ) .\nSince the cumulative distribution function is one to one, onto and continuous on [A, B], then the\ninverse function for P(x) exists. This “inversing” of variables gives the inverse method its name.\nr\nx A\nB A\n=\n−\n−\n,\nthen\nx = A + r (B – A).\nThe final equation is easy to use as a process generator for the uniform distribution. Given\nvalues for the parameters (A and B) and a random number on [0, 1], a sampled value (a vari-\nate) is obtained by substituting the appropriate values into the last equation. For example, if\nthe distribution of interest is uniformly distributed with a lower limit of 5 and an upper limit\nof 10 and the random number 0.75 is generated, the sampled value would be"],[36466,"chapter 36\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[58505,"Chapter 36 on the website for this book to find a level editor for this game and instruc-\ntions on how to make your own dungeons.\nComponent-Based Design\nFor this prototype, we'll attempt to work with component-based design as much as\npossible (see Chapter 27, \"Object-Oriented Thinking\"). To do this properly, you need to\nthink ahead a bit about how you want the game to work. The Legend of Zelda and most\nother games of the time were tile-based, meaning that their maps were constructed\nof a limited number of tiles that were repeated several times throughout the stage. In\nFigure 35.2, you can see the set of tiles you'll use on the left\n2\nand part of a map made\nfrom those tiles on the right.\nFigure 35.2 An atlas of tiles on the left and a room made from them on the right. A grid has been laid\nover the left image to help you see the edges of the tiles more clearly.\n1. Throughout this chapter, I'll use they, them, theirs pronouns to refer to Dray. Everyone\ndeserves to be represented in game development, in games, and in this book.\n2. The font that you see in the DelverTiles image is \"Octavius\" by Jack Oatley, co-member\nof Bitty/Brixdee. The font is 100% free to use. You can find 50 of their fonts at https://\nwww.dafont.com/profile.php?user=764521. His website is http://jack-oatley.com.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n1022 CHAPTER 35 DUNGEON DELVER – Part 1\nA few years ago, Unity introduced its own Tilemap engine, which is now part of the\nset of packages that is automatically included in the default 2D Unity project. Like\nmany new aspects of Unity, it can take some time to initially configure the Tilemap, but\nwhen it's working, it can save you both time and performance (it's much more effi-\ncient than the \"UniTile\" engine that I created for the second edition of this book). The\nUnity Tilemap engine doesn't normally pull in data from text files—as I'm doing in this\nchapter—so I'm using it a bit differently from the way Unity intended, but this is a good\nexample of how to modify Unity tech to suit your needs.\nUsing the Unity Tilemaps in this edition of the book has dictated some changes from the\nversion in the second edition, but we'll still focus on isolating code tasks as much as pos-\nsible. Here is the planned division of labor:\n1. MapInfo.cs: Understands the level and information about the tiles\n■ Read map data: MapInfo reads the DelverLevel_Eagle.txt map file to determine\nwhich tile is placed in each location on the map.\n■ Read collision data: MapInfo also reads the DelverCollisions.txt (and Delver-\nGrapTiles.txt) files to match up collision with the visuals of the tiles on the map.\n2. TilemapManager.cs: Uses MapInfo information to populate Unity Tilemaps\n■ Manage visual Tilemap: Pulls visual info from MapInfo and assigns the tiles on"],[36438,"Chapter 36 Random Variate Generation"],[36476,"chapter 37\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[36439,"Chapter 37 Testing for Randomness"],[57904,"Chapter 37—Coding Challenges\nThe Coding Challenges chapter introduces you to a type of project that you can find on\nthe book's website. One of my favorites is Updraft, a simple glider game challenge that\nI developed in 2020.\n2\nIn a Coding Challenge like Updraft, you will be presented with a\nstarter project and some—but not all—of the code for a simple game. This often includes\nall the named functions with parameters specified but with only comments (instead of\ncode) inside the function, describing what you need to write. The key concept here is to\ntransition your thought process from \"I'm following this tutorial\" to \"I want to do this—\nnow how do I make it happen?\" Coding Challenges are something completely new to\nthe third edition of the book, and I'm very excited to be giving them to you. I tend to\ndevelop a new challenge each semester and will continue to build the library of them\non the website for this book, under Chapter 37.\nGoing Beyond…\nAfter you've completed a few of the Coding Challenges, I encourage you to try what I\ncall a Classic Game Project. When I assign this to my students, they are asked to faithfully\nrecreate the mechanics and game feel of a classic game over the course of four weeks.\nSome great examples have included Metroid, Castlevania, MegaMan, and even Crazy\nTaxi. By being forced to work things out on their own, to schedule their own time, and\nto dig deeply into the inner workings of these seemingly simple games, the students\ncome to realize that they understand much more C# than they thought, and that is the\ntime that everything really falls into place. Another key element of the Classic Game\nProject is that you can figure out how to code the game without having to figure out the\ndesign of the game at the same time. At the end of this book, you will be prepared to\ntackle your own game projects (or your own Classic Game Project, if you want). Addi-\ntionally, the tutorials and challenges in this book can be a fantastic starting point on\nwhich to build your own games. For further information on continuing to improve your\ngame development practice after finishing this book, check out Chapter 38, \"Beyond\nThis Book.\"\n2. Huge thanks to my former teaching assistant Ellie Locatis, who in 2018 first proposed the\nconcept of these challenges as simple game projects to help prepare students to start\ndeveloping their own code.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n334 CHAPTER 17 IntrodUCInG UnIty hUB and the UnIty edItor\nYour Code Won't Look Like Mine\nOne last note:\n3\nThe code you come up with on your own is not going to look as clean as the code in this\nbook, and that is absolutely okay.\nI spent well over 1,000 hours writing the third edition of this book and working\nextremely hard to make my code as clean and readable as possible. When you first start"],[58848,"Chapter 37, \"Coding Challenges,\" covers these Coding Challenges in detail, but from\na pedagogical standpoint, I have found that having students work in pairs on these chal-\nlenges consistently improves their confidence in both themselves and their knowledge\nof game programming. This gives them a great place to start from for the final game\nproject.\nFinal Game Project\nThe last several weeks of class are devoted to the Final Game Project. Students can\neither work in pairs or solo on this project, and I require them to track their progress\nusing the burndown chart that is introduced in Chapter 14, \"The Agile Mentality.\" One\nof the toughest aspects of this project is helping the students decide on a project that\nis the right scope for the amount of time they have to spend on it. Most often, students\nchoose a project that is much more than they could accomplish in the little time that\nthey have, so I encourage them to start small with a concept that they can extend if they\nhave time.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute.\n16 A PPENDIX D Tips for Teaching f rom This Book\nClassic Game Project\nIn past semesters, I have not used some of the book tutorials in my class and have\ninstead devoted four weeks to a Classic Game Project and then only four weeks to the\nFinal Game project. In the Classic Game Project, students choose a game from the NES\nera and attempt to recreate the mechanics as faithfully as possible in Unity. This allows\nthe students to focus entirely on programming and implementation without also wor-\nrying about designing a game at the same time. One major caveat is that students will\nvery often spend far too much time on recreating the graphics of these classic games.\nEven when I tell them that graphics are worth only 5% of their grade on the project and\nthat I would be perfectly happy with a capsule jumping around as long as the physics\nof the jump were exactly like Samus in the original Metroid, they still often spend too\nmuch time on graphics.\nIn my current iteration of the class, I give students the option of creating their own new\ngame or recreating a classic game for their Final Game Project.\nMore Information Is Available\nI've collected more information about the classes I teach as well as some tools that I\nuse on the website for this book. Look for \"Appendix D\" on the http://book.prototools.\nnet website. As I mentioned at the top of this chapter, there is also a contact form on\nthe book's website where you can tell me about how you use this book in your classes.\nI would love to learn more about where and how it is being used. Thank you for teach-\ning from this book and for being someone who shares knowledge with others.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute."],[36509,"chapter 38\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[36440,"Chapter 38 Nonparametric Substitutes for Some Familiar\nParametric Tests"],[36531,"chapter 39\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[36441,"Chapter 39 Commonly Used Distributions"],[75152,"Chapter 4"],[60460,"CHAPTER 4"],[79228,"Chapter 4\nA Survey of Partitional and Hierarchical\nClustering Algorithms\nChandan K. Reddy\nWayne State University\nDetroit, MI\nreddy@cs.wayne.edu\nBhanukiran Vinzamuri\nWayne State University\nDetroit, MI\nbhanukiranv@wayne.edu\n4.1 Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 88\n4.2 Partitional Clustering Algorithms . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 89\n4.2.1 K -Means Clustering . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 89\n4.2.2 Minimization of Sum of Squared Errors . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 